.TH DD 1
.SH NAME
dd \- convert and copy a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "dd [ option=value ] ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dd
copies the specified input file to the specified output with possible conversions.
The standard input and output are used by default.
The input and output block size may be specified to take advantage of raw physical I/O, that is, to specify that transfers be done in chunks greater than the default 512 bytes.
.PP
.TP 12
.I option
.I values
.TP
.BI if=
input file name.
Standard input is default so you can also use
.B <
redirection
.TP
.BI of=
output file name
.I which must already exist.
Standard output is default; you can also use
.B >
redirection.
If
.B >
redirection is used, the output file is created if it does not exist, and if it does exist,
.I it is cleared.
.TP
.BI ibs= n
input block size
.I n
bytes (default 512)
.TP
.BI obs= n
output block size (default 512)
.TP
.BI bs= n
set both input and output block size, superseding
.B ibs
and
.BR obs ;
also, if no conversion is specified, it is particularly efficient since no copy need be done
.TP
.BI cbs= n
conversion buffer size
.TP
.BI skip= n
skip
.I n
input records before starting copy
.TP
.BI files= n
copy
.I n
files from (tape) input
.TP
.BI seek= n
seek
.I n
records from beginning of output file before copying
.TP
.BI count= n
copy only
.I n
input records
.TP
.BI conv= ascii
convert EBCDIC to ASCII
.TP
.B ebcdic
convert ASCII to EBCDIC
.TP
.B ibm
slightly different map of ASCII to EBCDIC
.TP
.B lcase
map alphabetics to lower case
.TP
.B ucase
map alphabetics to upper case
.TP
.B swab
swap every pair of bytes
.TP
.B noerror
do not stop processing on an error
.TP
.B sync
pad every input record to
.BR ibs
.TP
.B ... , ...
several comma-separated conversions
.PP
The record size (used for the
.BR skip ,
.B seek
and
.B count
options) is the same as the ``block'' size.
.PP
Where sizes are specified, a number of bytes is expected.
A number may end with
.BR k ,
.B b
or
.B w
to specify multiplication by 1024, 512, or 2 respectively; a pair of numbers may be separated by
.B x
to indicate a product.
All numbers are specified in decimal.
.PP
.B cbs
is used only if
.B ascii
or
.B ebcdic
conversion is specified.
In the former case
.B cbs
characters are placed into the conversion buffer, converted to ASCII, and trailing blanks trimmed and new-line added before sending the line to the output.
In the latter case ASCII characters are read into the conversion buffer, converted to EBCDIC, and blanks added to make up an output record of size
.BR cbs .
.PP
After completion,
.B dd
reports the number of whole and partial input and output blocks.
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy 640 blocks from floppy unit 0 to unit 1; use transfer size of 10 blocks:
.RS
.nf
dd if=/dev/f0 of=/dev/f1 bs=10b count=64
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cp(1), tr(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
f+p records in(out): numbers of full and partial records read(written)
.SH BUGS
The ASCII/EBCDIC conversion tables are taken from the 256 character standard in the CACM Nov, 1968.
.PP
Newlines are inserted only on conversion to ASCII; padding is done only on conversion to EBCDIC.
These should be separate options.
.sp
